{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_qwa6d56rel5jeezxn23terqhz2sguiebhvoebdj6jy5tbzlm6eja","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Translation complete and fully verified.\n\n## What the C library actually is\n\n`c_src/` contains exactly three files (`CMakeLists.txt`, `include/driver.h`, `src/driver.c`) — I confirmed with a recursive `find`, and the CMake target lists only `src/driver.c`. The whole library is one function.\n\nThe source is obfuscated with two rarely-used C features, which was the main translation hazard:\n\n- **Digraphs**: `%:` → `#`, `<%` → `{`, `%>` → `}`\n- **`<iso646.h>` alternative operator spellings**: `bitor` → `|`, `compl` → `~`\n\nDe-obfuscated, the entire library is:\n\n```c\nvoid driver(int x, int y) { int result = x | ~y; printf(\"%d\", result); puts(\"\"); }\n```\n\n## Completion gate — symbol parity\n\nThe C `.so`'s complete public ABI is a single defined symbol:\n\n```\n0000000000001119 T driver     (plus only U printf/puts imports and w weak stubs)\n```\n\n`nm -D` diff between the C and Rust `.so` is **empty** — exact match, verified again after a clean from-scratch rebuild. No namespace-renaming macros exist in the header, so the linker symbol is plainly `driver`.\n\n## Byte-identical output\n\nTwo differential harnesses `dlopen`ed each `.so` and compared raw stdout:\n\n| Test | Result |\n|---|---|\n| 529 pairs incl. `INT_MIN`, `INT_MAX`, `±1`, `0x55555555` | identical, md5 `0060b0ff…` |\n| 50,000 random pairs + interleaved host C stdio writes | identical, md5 `c7353037…` |\n\nOne deliberate design decision: I call libc's `printf`/`puts` via `extern \"C\"` rather than Rust's `print!`. Rust's `std::io::Stdout` keeps its **own** buffer, separate from the C `FILE *stdout` buffer, so a host program mixing its own C stdio with library calls would see reordered output. The interleaving test above (`HOST:` / `HOSTLINE` markers between `driver` calls, block-buffered to a pipe) specifically exercises this and matches byte-for-byte. This also avoided any external crate, which matters since the sandbox has no network — the crate builds `--offline` with zero dependencies.\n\nAlso worth noting: `x | ~y` involves no overflow (bitwise ops on 32-bit two's-complement), so Rust `i32` semantics match C exactly with no wrapping-arithmetic workaround needed. No bugs were \"fixed\" — e.g. the odd `printf` + `puts(\"\")` split is preserved verbatim rather than collapsed into one `\"%d\\n\"`.\n\nFiles written (nothing in `c_src/` was modified — mtimes still `21:13` vs. my writes at `21:14`): `Cargo.toml` (`crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]`) and `src/lib.rs`. `cargo build --release` succeeds with no warnings."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":624,"cache_read_input_tokens":25435,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":624,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"58834e88-1b55-4d24-942c-44555d910b13","uuid":"926196d2-5ca3-469c-87d1-92f8ddc70e5f","timestamp":"2026-08-17T21:15:39.408Z"}
